# Rule: the sheet (ADR-114)

The canonical rule is `.claude/rules/sheet.md`; this file mirrors it for Cursor.

- A section is ONE arrangement (`SHEET_ARRANGEMENTS`); chrome strings are
  lettered by renderers, never authored. A new kind is a union member, a
  renderer case and a rubric row in one commit.
- The variety law is code (`lib/sheet/composition.ts`) and every real page
  walks it in `tests/lib/sheet-composition.test.ts`. The chapter cap is on the
  PRIMARY row.
- `sheet.css` is token-only, pinned at zero in three ratchets; light re-derives
  every `--sh-*` alpha in `theme.css`.
- No vertical rule of the page's own: the frame's rails are the only verticals
  (ADR-114 U1); a section divides itself with seams and shared cell edges.
- The first value of every knob is the house; `lib/sheet/directions.json` is
  the one registry, mirrored into the ship's `armada.toml` and asserted.
- Every client-console readout is DERIVED from the arcs registry.
- `/arcs` is NOT a document (ADR-118): it is the owner's instrument, a
  `monitor` then a `log`, judged by `instrumentViolations` (every mark at
  its own date on both windows, the lit mark IS the selected row, one id
  set across marks, rows and dossiers). Both windows are rendered and
  `.sh-t` picks one, because `span` is a client-side knob.
- Each device is as tall as the rails; the graticule is DOM (never a
  `viewBox`) and is the one named exemption to the rails-only verticals.
- The log is the references' composition since ADR-118 U2: the kinds DIVIDE
  the list (a head per kind, level with the dossier's band — the filter is
  deleted), each block is a kind-of-page icon (ink, never gold) beside a plate
  notched bottom-left with the client as its title over the bracketed
  engagement and the date. A bracket is never stored. `--log-block-h` divides
  the device above a 50px floor — declared on the LIST, where the server
  writes `--log-n` and `--log-heads` (on the root it goes invalid silently);
  under the floor the list runs past the screen beside the sticky dossier.
  The sections run by newest filing; the monitor's lanes keep registry order.
  The `rows` (SH) and `dossier` (SJ) knobs are deleted.
- The dossier reads like travel data (ADR-118 U3): the band in the proof
  card's folder tint (gold .28 → a quarter, light .22; the smoke composites
  it under each band word, because the contrast walks cannot see a
  gradient), a READOUT of framed keys (filled with `--sh-well`) with their
  values framed beside them and set right, and one full-width button (the
  proof card's `.pf-watch`). The brief's sentence and the printed key hints
  are gone; the keys still work.
- The dossier draws the client's intelligence configuration, proposals only:
  the BOARD (`components/sheet/config/`, server SVG — a Tensor-gold die of
  workstreams with its words on `--gold-contrast`, gold legs, amber wires,
  gold-ink kind codes on dawn chips). Links are DERIVED from each workstream's
  own `where` / `runs` (`lib/arcs/stack.ts`), pinned per proposal in
  `sheet-config-fit`. Four crops, a container query picks one (≤ 10 %
  letterbox); fit is declared and the type floor is asserted against measured
  boxes, on every proposal. Every evenodd ring closes BOTH contours, or it
  paints a bow-tie.
- The frame's wordmark is DOCKED from the first frame on the instrument: the
  hero lockup sat 7px under the monitor's corner. The smoke asserts
  clearance, not order ("under the device" passed at 7px).
- `SheetInstrumentController` is the ONE client file and imports NO
  registry — a client chunk is public, the page is not (ADR-117);
  `arcs-import-doctrine` fails any client file under the sheet that does.
- `data-dos-id` on the root is the observable: written only when the
  incoming dossier's aperture has opened, and on arrival at once — a deep
  link to the already-chosen row included. (Until U2 it also waited on the
  dossier's picture, which is gone.) Motion is clips, never opacity.
- The console imports `useStackedCardsScroll` and copies the proof stack's
  skin as `--sh-*` tokens; `.sh-root` is `overflow-x: clip`.
- `data-sh-ready` is the capture's observable; run the capture and the
  mechanical gate from PowerShell (MSYS rewrites a `/route` argument).
- A reveal in the viewport's last tenth never lands; the capture waits on 0.88.
- The negative pole is shot once and never re-shot; a wave without
  `ledger.py tick --handback` never happened.
